When working with machine learning models, it's important to understand capacity, overfitting, underfitting, and generalization as they 
directly influence a model's ability to learn and make predictions on new, unseen data.  
1. Capacity: 
Capacity refers to the ability of a model to fit a variety of functions or patterns in the data. It is primarily determined by the complexity of the 
model, such as the number of parameters, layers in a neural network, or the degree of a polynomial in polynomial regression. A model with 
high capacity can learn complex patterns, while a model with low capacity may struggle to learn even simple patterns. 
 High Capacity: Models like deep neural networks or very flexible models like decision trees with a lot of depth have a high 
capacity to fit data. 
 Low Capacity: Models like linear regression or simple decision trees have lower capacity and are more constrained in terms of 
their ability to fit complex patterns. 
2. Overfitting: 
Overfitting occurs when a model learns the training data too well, including the noise and random fluctuations, which leads to poor 
performance on new, unseen data (testing or validation set). Essentially, the model "memorizes" the training data rather than learning the 
general underlying patterns. 
 Symptoms of Overfitting: 
o High accuracy on the training set. 
o Low accuracy on the test set or validation set. 
o The model captures noise or irrelevant details that don't generalize. 
 Common Causes of Overfitting: 
o A model with too high capacity relative to the amount of data (e.g., a complex neural network trained on a small dataset). 
o Too many features or noise in the data. 
o Insufficient regularization. 
 Solutions to Overfitting: 
o Use simpler models (lower capacity). 
o Regularization techniques (e.g., L1/L2 regularization, dropout). 
o Use more training data. 
o Cross-validation to ensure generalizability. 
