3. Underfitting: 
Underfitting occurs when a model is too simple to capture the underlying patterns in the data. This typically happens when the model has too 
low capacity, meaning it cannot represent the complexity of the data, and thus performs poorly on both the training set and the test set. 
 Symptoms of Underfitting: 
o Low accuracy on both training and test sets. 
o The model doesn't capture important relationships in the data. 
 Common Causes of Underfitting: 
o The model is too simple (e.g., using linear regression when the data has complex, non-linear relationships). 
o Insufficient training (e.g., not enough epochs or iterations in training). 
o Not enough features or relevant data. 
 Solutions to Underfitting: 
o Increase model complexity (e.g., use a more complex algorithm or add more features). 
o Train the model for longer. 
o Try different types of models that might capture more complex patterns. 
4. Generalization: 
Generalization is the model’s ability to perform well on unseen data, i.e., its ability to generalize the patterns learned from the training set to 
new data. A model that generalizes well is one that strikes a balance between underfitting and overfitting. 
 Good Generalization: A model that performs well on both the training set and the test set. It has learned the underlying patterns in 
the data without memorizing specific details that don’t apply to new data. 
 Bad Generalization: If a model performs well on the training set but poorly on the test set, it has likely overfitted the training data. 
Conversely, if the model performs poorly on both sets, it may be underfitting. 
Balancing Capacity, Overfitting, Underfitting, and Generalization: 
 Bias-Variance Tradeoff: This is a fundamental concept that explains the relationship between underfitting (high bias) and 
overfitting (high variance). A model with high bias tends to underfit, while a model with high variance tends to overfit.  
o Bias: The error introduced by approximating a real-world problem with a simplified model. High bias can lead to 
underfitting. 
o Variance: The error introduced by the model's sensitivity to small fluctuations in the training data. High variance can lead 
to overfitting. 
Visualization: 
 Underfitting: A model that is too simple might look like a straight line trying to fit a complex, curved dataset. 
 Overfitting: A model that is too complex might bend and twist to fit every data point in the training set, even the noise. 
 Good Generalization: A model that finds the underlying trend of the data, capturing the overall pattern without fitting to noise or 
unnecessary details. 
